---
/**
 * foodcraft / HeaderBN — THE STICKY MASTHEAD. "Panday Coffee Works"'s front
 * gate, reproduced from the design's own <header>: a 95%-opaque bone bar over a
 * 12px backdrop blur, ruled beneath in FULL-STRENGTH ink, and split by a
 * `minmax(0,1fr) auto` grid into a name lock on the left and a run of ruled
 * mono cells on the right — five 10px wayfinding cells at 0/17px padding, each
 * opened by a --line hairline, and the rust CTA cell closing the row.
 *
 * ROLE: wayfinding, plus the one action this page exists for. Nothing else.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS — every visible string on this bar, and nothing else:
 *   footer.brand                     — the 19px uppercase wordmark
 *   footer.locality                  — the mono place tag on its right
 *   navbar_links.${i}.label          — one wayfinding cell's label
 *   navbar_links.${i}.href           — that cell's target (data-href-field)
 *   navCtaText                       — the rust cell's label
 *   navCtaHref                       — the rust cell's target (data-href-field)
 * All six are declared in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts under the
 * `header` group, so each is a hook a sidebar input actually owns. A data-field
 * on any other path would be a dead hook nothing can edit.
 *
 * DERIVED, never authored here:
 *   - the wordmark falls back to layout.businessName when content.footer.brand
 *     is blank — the same name from the same submission, and the same fallback
 *     the schema already declares (footer.brand fallbackPaths ['business_name']),
 *     so the sidebar and the page cannot disagree about what it says.
 *   - every in-page href is resolved by onPage() against the ids PageBN really
 *     renders. Off-page targets (tel:, mailto:, http(s)) pass through untouched.
 *   - `i` is the ORIGINAL, pre-filter index of a nav row, so dropping an
 *     unreachable row 2 cannot misdirect an edit into row 3.
 *   - target/rel on the CTA are COMPUTED from its own href, never literal, and
 *     each is one conditional, so neither attribute can be emitted twice.
 *
 * NOT RENDERED, deliberately:
 *   - THE DESIGN'S OWN COPY. "Panday Coffee Works", "Roastery & bar — Escolta,
 *     Manila", "Works", "Prices", "Gallery", "Location", "Notes" and "Contact
 *     us" are the DESIGNER filling in their own template. Not one of those
 *     words is in this file. A roastery whose owner supplied no nav rows gets
 *     no nav rows, not the designer's five.
 *   - THE PREFIX OF THE DESIGN'S PLACE TAG. Its `shopMeta` is the literal
 *     string 'Roastery & bar — ' concatenated with the town. Only the TOWN is
 *     owner data, so only the town is printed: footer.locality, alone, in the
 *     designer's exact mono metrics. Printing the prefix would put a trade
 *     description on a business that never claimed it.
 *   - contact.phone. It is in the `header` schema group, but this design's
 *     header has no phone cell — the design puts the number in the FOOTER, and
 *     inventing a sixth cell for it is not this file's call.
 *   - business_name / tagline. They drive the DOCUMENT (title, description,
 *     og:*), which PageBN owns. Nothing on this bar reads them except the
 *     wordmark's fallback above.
 *   - THE WHOLE BAR, when brand, place tag, nav rows and CTA are ALL empty. A
 *     sticky bar with nothing in it is ~50px of dead chrome on every screen and
 *     a rule under nothing. It is BODY content that is counted — never a tag or
 *     a headline, because lib/derive-content-defaults.ts fills those on every
 *     site and a gate that counted them would always pass. This bar carries no
 *     tag and no headline at all, so the gate is simply everything it can draw.
 *   - the burger, unless there is a drawer for it to open.
 *   - the wordmark, with no name. 'Your Business' once shipped as a real
 *     customer's own wordmark; with nothing supplied the place tag carries the
 *     lock alone, and with neither the lock is gone entirely.
 *   - a nav row with no label, or whose href reaches no section on this page.
 *     A 0-width tap target and a link that scrolls nowhere are both broken.
 *   Every guard sits OUTSIDE its tag, so a blank value removes the ELEMENT and
 *   not just its text — no empty rust cell, no hairline opening a cell that is
 *   not there, no lock with nothing in it.
 *   There is NO star row, rating or numeral anywhere on this bar, so nothing
 *   here can print a claim the owner did not make. There is no upload either,
 *   hence no role="img" and no image alt text.
 *
 * DECISIONS A READER MIGHT OTHERWISE TAKE FOR AN OVERSIGHT
 *
 * 1. NO .rail AND NO ROTATED INDEX LABEL HERE. The spine is the signature of
 *    this template, but the design does not put the header on it: its <header>
 *    is a `minmax(0,1fr) auto` bar, not the `minmax(0,52px) minmax(0,1fr)`
 *    section grid, and it carries no index. So this file emits neither .sec nor
 *    .rail. What it DOES take from the spine is the rule: its bottom border is
 *    1px --rule-strong — the same full-strength ink every .sec is ruled with,
 *    NOT --line — so the sticky bar reads as the first rule of the sheet rather
 *    than as chrome parked above it. The design says exactly that (#17181A).
 *
 * 2. THE NAV IS A ROW OF RULED CELLS, NOT A GAPPED LINK LIST. The design gives
 *    each link `padding:0 17px` and `border-left:1px solid #C8C7C2` with no
 *    gap, and stretches every cell to the full bar height. That is what makes
 *    the masthead read as a technical document; a gap would turn it into an
 *    ordinary nav. align-items:stretch is therefore load-bearing all the way
 *    down the chain — grid, .pcbn-right, .pcbn-nav — and the CTA is the last
 *    cell in the same run, which is why it needs no special height.
 *
 * 3. THE CTA IS THE WRAPPER'S .btn .btn--fill, UNMODIFIED. The design's btnNav
 *    is mono 10px caps at .14em with 15px/24px padding on an accent fill with
 *    an --on-accent label — every one of those values is the wrapper's .btn +
 *    .btn--fill. So nothing about the box is restated at desktop: no fill, no
 *    label colour, no padding, no font size. The wrapper's hover darkening and
 *    focus ring stay intact for free.
 *    THREE VALUES THE PRIMITIVE ADDS, none of them restated away:
 *      - font-weight 500. btnNav declares none, so the DESIGN's CTA renders at
 *        the inherited 400. The wrapper sets 500 on every .btn, and the design's
 *        other three buttons (btnSolid/btnOrange/btnCream) declare no weight
 *        either — so 400 here would make this bar the one button on the page
 *        that is lighter than the rest. The family primitive wins.
 *      - min-height 44px, which never binds: the CTA is align-self:stretch in a
 *        bar the 13/26 lock already drives past 44px.
 *      - a 1px accent border on all four sides where btnNav draws only
 *        `border-left:1px solid accent`. Accent on an accent fill, so the
 *        rendered colour is identical; the box is 1px wider on three sides.
 *
 * 4. WHY THE CTA HOOKS navCtaText/navCtaHref AND NOT hero.cta1.*.
 *    components/editor/editorBridge.ts resolves a Blocks toggle to a section by
 *    counting hooks of that prefix, so a header carrying hero.* hooks would
 *    vanish when the owner switched the Hero block off. navCta* belongs to the
 *    Header group, so this bar answers only to itself. footer.brand is safe
 *    twice over: the bridge only ever hides `closest('section,footer')` and a
 *    <header> is neither, and the real footer outweighs this file's two
 *    footer.* hooks many times over. The CTA does NOT borrow the hero's label
 *    for its DISPLAY either — showing a string the Header-CTA box does not
 *    contain means the sidebar and the page disagree about what the button
 *    says. An empty box renders no cell.
 *
 * 5. THE NAV ROWS ARE READ FROM content.navbar_links, NOT layout.navLinks, AND
 *    THAT IS A DELIBERATE DEPARTURE FROM THE PREVIOUS TEMPLATE IN THIS FAMILY.
 *    lib/astro-builder.ts fills `content.navbar_links` from the owner's rows or
 *    else from derive-content-defaults' five, but it builds `layout.navLinks`
 *    from the RAW `content.navbar_links || [four hard-coded rows]` in the same
 *    return object — so on a site whose owner supplied no nav links the two
 *    arrays are DIFFERENT LENGTHS with different labels. The sidebar list edits
 *    `navbar_links`, so `navbar_links.${i}` is only a live hook when `i` indexes
 *    THAT array. layout.navLinks is kept as a defensive fallback for a harness
 *    that renders this component without going through astro-builder.
 *
 * 6. WHY THE CELL HOVER IS --accent-deep. It is the design's OWN hover: its
 *    stylesheet declares a:hover{color:#7C2412}, which is --accent-deep. In the
 *    demo that rule is shadowed by the inline colour on each nav cell, so the
 *    designer's page happens to render no hover change — an artefact of inline
 *    specificity, not a decision. Shipping a five-cell row with no hover state
 *    would be an interaction regression dressed as fidelity.
 *
 * 7. THE BURGER AND DRAWER ARE AN ADDITION, NOT A RE-DERIVATION. The design is
 *    a desktop artboard and specifies no mobile masthead at all; five mono
 *    cells plus a CTA need roughly 530px of run and would push a 360px page
 *    sideways. So the cell row becomes a drawer at ONE width (<=900px) — the
 *    row leaves and the burger arrives together, so there is never a band where
 *    neither is on screen — and nothing in the desktop rendering moves. The
 *    burger is not a new shape: it is another ruled cell, opened by the same
 *    --line hairline as the wayfinding cells, with no fill and no radius. The
 *    drawer's ground is OPAQUE --paper rather than the bar's 95%, because it
 *    hangs over live page content and a translucent panel would print the page
 *    through the links; it is told apart from the page the way this design
 *    tells everything apart — by rules and a shadow, never by a fill.
 *
 * 8. THE DRAWER REDRAWS THE FOCUS RING INSET, AT <=900px ONLY. The drawer is a
 *    scroll container (max-height + overflow-y, and `overflow-y:auto` forces
 *    overflow-x to `auto` too), so it clips its children's OUTSET box-shadow
 *    and outline at its own padding box — the wrapper's ring is 6px of accent
 *    outside the border edge, and each drawer row is exactly the drawer's
 *    width, so on a phone the ring would have been clipped on both sides of
 *    every row. The same two-colour ring is therefore redrawn with an inset
 *    shadow and a negative outline-offset, inside the row where nothing can
 *    clip it. THE ARITHMETIC, because the offset has to be exact for the ring
 *    to read the same way round:
 *      wrapper — box-shadow spread 6px accent covers 0..+6px outside the border
 *      box; the 3px paper outline at offset 0 paints on top of 0..+3px. Read
 *      outward-in that is 3px accent, then 3px paper against the row.
 *      drawer  — inset shadow spread 6px accent covers 0..-6px inside; the 3px
 *      paper outline at offset -6px sits at -6..-3px, i.e. it covers the INNER
 *      half. Read outward-in: 3px accent, then 3px paper. Same two colours,
 *      same two widths, same order.
 *      (offset -3px would have put the paper on the OUTSIDE and mirrored the
 *      ring; an audit caught exactly that here.)
 *    Desktop keeps the wrapper's ring untouched. (The height cap is kept rather
 *    than dropped: a drawer that runs past the fold with no way to reach its
 *    last link is the worse bug.)
 *
 * 9. THE MOBILE CTA WRAPS, IT DOES NOT ELLIPSIS. From 900px down — the same
 *    width at which the nav leaves the bar and the CTA becomes the only elastic
 *    thing on it — the CTA takes `flex: 0 1 auto` plus BOTH `white-space:
 *    normal` AND `overflow-wrap: anywhere`. The pair, never one: a label with no
 *    spaces escapes its box when only white-space is set. It is set at 900 and
 *    not at 520 because between those two widths the CTA is otherwise
 *    `nowrap` and unshrinkable, and the grid's first track is minmax(0,1fr) —
 *    a long owner label would crush the lock to zero and then run under the
 *    wrapper's `overflow-x: hidden`, losing its own tail. Below 520 the cap on
 *    its width lands on top (46vw, then 40vw). Truncating instead would hide
 *    words the owner wrote; a two-line cell that shows all of them is the
 *    lesser cost on a bar this short. (The WORDMARK does ellipsis, and that is
 *    not the same call: a wordmark that wraps stops being a lock and grows a
 *    sticky bar on every scroll.)
 *
 * 10. NO clamp() ANYWHERE IN THIS FILE, AND NO TYPE THAT GROWS AS THE VIEWPORT
 *     NARROWS. Every type value is a fixed px or em that only ever steps DOWN:
 *     wordmark 19 -> 17; lock padding 13/26 -> 12/18 -> 12/14; CTA 15/24 ->
 *     12/14 -> 12/11 and .14em -> .1em; place tag .16em -> .12em -> hidden;
 *     burger 46px -> 42px. The nav-cell type and the CTA type hold 10px at
 *     EVERY width — the 48px drawer target is bought with the ROW, never by
 *     growing type. With no clamps and a ladder monotonic in one direction, the
 *     floor-under-a-cap inversion two earlier audits found is not expressible.
 *     THE ONE VALUE THAT STEPS UP, stated so it is not read as that inversion:
 *     a nav cell's padding goes 0/17 -> 14/26 at <=900 -> 14/18 at <=520. At
 *     900 the cell stops being a cell in a horizontal run and becomes a
 *     full-width drawer ROW, and 26px is the lock's own gutter, so the rows
 *     line up under the wordmark instead of hanging off it. A full-width row
 *     cannot push sideways whatever its padding — and it carries
 *     overflow-wrap: anywhere for the label itself.
 *
 * 11. THE TWO INVISIBLE AUTHORED STRINGS are "Primary" on the nav landmark and
 *     "Menu" on the icon-only burger. They are a11y affordances in the same
 *     class as the wrapper's skip link, never page copy. The burger's three
 *     bars are decorative and carry a LITERAL aria-hidden; nothing on this bar
 *     carries a conditional one, so no element can end up with the attribute
 *     twice. The lock gets no aria-label: its accessible name is its own text,
 *     the owner's name and the owner's town, and appending a literal like
 *     "home" would announce a word this file never received.
 *
 * CONTRAST, computed for this file against this design's palette (sRGB
 * relative luminance, WCAG 2.x). Note the bar is 95% --paper over whatever
 * scrolls beneath it, so the effective ground is very slightly modulated by
 * the page; the figures are against --paper itself:
 *   --ink on --paper                15.30  (wordmark)
 *   --ink-2 on --paper               9.23  (nav cells, rest state)
 *   --accent-deep on --paper         8.54  (nav cells, hover)
 *   --ink-soft on --paper            5.77  (the place tag)
 *   --on-accent on an --accent fill  5.67  (the CTA label)
 * Every one clears AA for normal text; the tightest is the CTA label at 5.67.
 * These are this file's own arithmetic, and where they overlap PageBN's table
 * they REPRODUCE it exactly (ink/paper 15.30, ink-soft/paper 5.77,
 * accent/paper 5.67). The wrapper's `.plate .cap` comment agrees as well: it
 * reads 15.30:1 for --on-ink (#EFEEEA) on --ink (#17181A), which is the
 * ink/paper pair inverted. (An earlier draft of that comment said 14.4; it has
 * since been corrected at the wrapper, so there is nothing to report here.)
 *
 * Never visibility-gated in this file: PageBN owns `visibility.headerSection`.
 *
 * CLASS PREFIX: every scoped class is `pcbn-`, unique to this template. An
 * audit had to rename a whole set of classes copied verbatim from a sibling;
 * nothing here shares a name with HeaderBM's `tcbm-` or HeaderBL's `hb-`.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content, layout } = Astro.props;
const c = (content as any) || {};
const lay = (layout as any) || {};

// ── ANCHOR GUARD ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// lib/derive-content-defaults.ts hard-codes nav hrefs to '#about', '#services',
// '#why', '#work', '#visit' and navCtaHref to '#visit', and lib/astro-builder.ts
// falls back to '#about', '#services', '#gallery', '#contact' — ids most designs
// never render, so those links scrolled nowhere. Resolve every in-page href
// against what PageBN ACTUALLY renders: keep it when the id exists, remap it
// when this design carries the same section under another name, otherwise fall
// back (nav rows pass '' and are dropped). Off-page hrefs — tel:, mailto:,
// http(s) and anything else without a leading '#' — pass through untouched.
const PAGE_ANCHORS = new Set(['menu', 'about', 'gallery', 'visit', 'faq', 'main']);
// This design renames five derived targets: the services block IS the price
// list, so '#services' (and '#rooms') land on '#menu'; the roastery's own story
// absorbs both '#host' and '#why'; '#work' is the photo wall; '#location' and
// '#contact' both mean the visit block — address, map and opening hours. A
// booking link has no checkout to reach on this page, so it lands on <main>.
const ANCHOR_ALIAS: Record<string, string> = {
  book: 'main',
  contact: 'visit',
  host: 'about',
  location: 'visit',
  rooms: 'menu',
  services: 'menu',
  why: 'about',
  work: 'gallery',
};
const onPage = (href: string | undefined, fallback: string): string => {
  if (!href) return fallback;
  if (!href.startsWith('#')) return href;
  const id = href.slice(1);
  if (PAGE_ANCHORS.has(id)) return href;
  const alias = ANCHOR_ALIAS[id];
  return alias && PAGE_ANCHORS.has(alias) ? `#${alias}` : fallback;
};

// The wordmark. 'Your Business' was a shipped placeholder that rendered as a
// real customer's own wordmark; with nothing supplied the mark is omitted.
// `||`, never `??`: genericContentSchema gives footer.brand fallbackPaths
// ['business_name'] and ContentFieldsAuto's readWithFallbacks treats '' as
// empty, so a CLEARED brand box still displays the business name in the
// sidebar. With `??` an empty string would be a value and the page would go
// wordmark-less while the sidebar showed a name.
const brand = String(c.footer?.brand || lay.businessName || '').trim();
// The place tag. One field, several consumers across the page, and nothing
// derives it — on a first build it is simply absent rather than guessed out of
// the address.
const locality = String(c.footer?.locality ?? '').trim();

// Nav rows. See decision 5: content.navbar_links is the array the sidebar list
// edits, so it is the array `i` must index. `i` is the ORIGINAL index, captured
// before the filter, so per-row edits stay aimed at the row the owner clicked
// even after unreachable rows are dropped.
const navSource: any[] = Array.isArray(c.navbar_links)
  ? c.navbar_links
  : (Array.isArray(lay.navLinks) ? lay.navLinks : []);
const navLinks = navSource
  .map((l: any, i: number) => ({ label: String(l?.label ?? '').trim(), href: onPage(l?.href, ''), i }))
  .filter((l: { href: string; label: string; i: number }) => Boolean(l.href && l.label));

// The rust cell. Label and target both come from the Header group; an empty
// label removes the whole element rather than leaving a rust rectangle.
const ctaText = String(c.navCtaText ?? '').trim();
// '#visit' is the fallback because that is where this design puts the address,
// the map and the opening hours — the page has no checkout to send a booking
// link to, and it is the id the derived default already names.
const ctaHref = onPage(c.navCtaHref, '#visit');
const ctaExternal = /^https?:/i.test(ctaHref);

// The bar renders only when it CARRIES something. All four counted values are
// body content; there is no tag and no headline on this bar for a derived
// default to fake the gate with.
const hasRail = Boolean(brand || locality || navLinks.length > 0 || ctaText);
---
{hasRail && (
  <header class="pcbn-rail">
    <div class="pcbn-grid">
      {(brand || locality) && (
        <a class="pcbn-lock" href="#main">
          {brand && <span class="pcbn-mark" data-field="footer.brand">{brand}</span>}
          {locality && <span class="pcbn-meta" data-field="footer.locality">{locality}</span>}
        </a>
      )}
      {(navLinks.length > 0 || ctaText) && (
        <div class="pcbn-right">
          {navLinks.length > 0 && (
            <nav class="pcbn-nav" id="pcbn-nav-BN" aria-label="Primary">
              {navLinks.map((link) => (
                <a
                  href={link.href}
                  data-field={`navbar_links.${link.i}.label`}
                  data-href-field={`navbar_links.${link.i}.href`}
                >{link.label}</a>
              ))}
            </nav>
          )}
          {/* OUTSIDE .pcbn-nav on purpose: .pcbn-nav becomes a drawer at
              <=900px and the one action this template exists for must not
              leave the bar with it. */}
          {ctaText && (
            <a
              href={ctaHref}
              class="btn btn--fill pcbn-cta"
              target={ctaExternal ? '_blank' : undefined}
              rel={ctaExternal ? 'noopener noreferrer' : undefined}
              data-field="navCtaText"
              data-href-field="navCtaHref"
            >{ctaText}</a>
          )}
          {navLinks.length > 0 && (
            <button class="pcbn-burger" type="button" aria-label="Menu" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="pcbn-nav-BN">
              <span aria-hidden="true"></span><span aria-hidden="true"></span><span aria-hidden="true"></span>
            </button>
          )}
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  </header>
)}
<script is:inline>
  (function () {
    // Null-guarded rather than conditionally rendered: an is:inline script must
    // stay at the top level of the component, and with no bar (or no drawer) it
    // simply exits. 'pcbn-rail' is template-unique because a preview harness
    // can render two wrappers into one document and a shared class would
    // cross-wire the drawers.
    var rail = document.querySelector('header.pcbn-rail');
    if (!rail) return;
    var btn = rail.querySelector('.pcbn-burger');
    if (!btn) return;
    function setOpen(open) {
      rail.classList.toggle('is-open', open);
      btn.setAttribute('aria-expanded', open ? 'true' : 'false');
    }
    btn.addEventListener('click', function () { setOpen(!rail.classList.contains('is-open')); });
    // '.pcbn-cta' is in this selector because the CTA lives OUTSIDE the drawer:
    // tapping it while the drawer is open must still close the drawer.
    rail.querySelectorAll('.pcbn-nav a, .pcbn-cta').forEach(function (a) {
      a.addEventListener('click', function () { setOpen(false); });
    });
    document.addEventListener('keydown', function (e) { if (e.key === 'Escape') setOpen(false); });
  })();
</script>
<style>
  /* The design: sticky, 95% bone, blur(12px), ruled beneath in full-strength
     ink. The tint is a straight rgba() on --paper-rgb, which PageBN publishes
     and buildOverrideCss remaps with the rest of the palette, so the design's
     own rgba(239,238,234,.95) is expressible with no literal in it.
     (Never write a literal end-style tag inside this block, comments included:
      it is an HTML raw-text element and the parser would end the stylesheet
      right there, silently truncating the file.) */
  .pcbn-rail {
    /* z-index is the design's own 50. The wrapper's skip link sits at 100, so
       the bar clears the page and the skip link still clears the bar. */
    position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 50;
    background-color: rgba(var(--paper-rgb), .95);
    /* --rule-strong, NOT --line: see decision 1. */
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
    -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(12px);
    backdrop-filter: blur(12px);
  }

  /* The design's own two-column bar. align-items:stretch is what makes the nav
     cells full-height; the explicit grid-column on each child keeps the lock
     left and the cells right even in the degenerate cases the design never drew
     (a shop with no name, or with no nav and no CTA). */
  .pcbn-grid {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
    align-items: stretch;
  }

  /* The name lock: 13px/26px padding at a 14px gap, ink, exactly as drawn. The
     44px floor only ever binds when the wordmark is absent and the place tag
     carries the lock alone — with a wordmark the design's own metrics already
     compute taller than a legal tap target. */
  .pcbn-lock {
    grid-column: 1;
    display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 14px;
    padding: 13px 26px; min-width: 0; min-height: 44px;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .pcbn-mark {
    font-family: var(--disp); font-weight: 800; font-size: 19px;
    letter-spacing: -.02em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink);
    /* line-height is the DESIGN'S value: it states none, so its wordmark
       renders at `normal`. It is RESTATED rather than left to inherit because
       the wrapper's body sets 1.62 and 19px of Archivo would sit in a 31px line
       box, inflating the whole bar. It must not be TIGHTENED either: `normal`
       is the face's own ascent+descent, and a smaller line box is clipped by
       the overflow:hidden the ellipsis below requires — shaving the tail off a
       descender and the tilde off a capital (Niño). */
    line-height: normal;
    /* A long single-word shop name must not push the CTA off a 360px screen;
       flex items are blockified, so ellipsis applies here. */
    min-width: 0; overflow: hidden; white-space: nowrap; text-overflow: ellipsis;
  }
  /* NOT the wrapper's .mono primitive: that is 500 weight at .2em in --muted,
     and the design's tag is 400 at .16em in --ink-soft. Close enough to be
     mistaken for it, far enough to be visibly wrong if borrowed. */
  .pcbn-meta {
    flex: 0 1 auto; min-width: 0;
    font-family: var(--mono); font-weight: 400; font-size: 10px;
    letter-spacing: .16em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-soft);
    line-height: normal;
    overflow: hidden; white-space: nowrap; text-overflow: ellipsis;
  }

  /* The right-hand run. No gap and no padding of its own: the extra flex level
     exists only so the wayfinding cells can be a <nav> landmark that the drawer
     can move without taking the CTA and the burger with it. Desktop pixels are
     identical to the design's single flex row. */
  .pcbn-right { grid-column: 2; display: flex; align-items: stretch; min-width: 0; }
  .pcbn-nav { display: flex; align-items: stretch; min-width: 0; }
  .pcbn-nav a {
    display: flex; align-items: center;
    padding: 0 17px;
    border-left: 1px solid var(--line);
    font-family: var(--mono); font-weight: 400; font-size: 10px;
    letter-spacing: .14em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-2);
